 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">1. The Short Answer: UKCA vs CE Charger Rules for 2026</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-700 font-semibold leading-relaxed mb-4">Direct answer: CE is still accepted in Great Britain, and UKCA is voluntary for most chargers. The 31 December 2027 date is a labelling easement, not a CE deadline. Northern Ireland is different: CE is required, and UKCA alone isn't accepted.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>CE</strong> is the EU conformity mark. In Great Britain, CE is recognised for the 21 product regulations covered by the 2024 amendment. In Northern Ireland, CE is the required mark.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>UKCA</strong> is the UK Conformity Assessed mark. It remains valid for Great Britain, but it isn't the only route for most covered products. It isn't accepted as the sole mark in Northern Ireland or the EU.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>UKNI</strong> isn't a standalone mark. It appears with CE when a UK body carried out mandatory third-party conformity assessment for Northern Ireland. CE plus UKNI cannot be sold in the EU or EEA.</p>

 <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl">
 <table class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl text-sm overflow-hidden">
 <thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white">
 <tr><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Market</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Accepted Marking</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">What the Importer Needs</th></tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Great Britain; consumer charger; self-declared; no radio</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">CE or UKCA; dual marking allowed</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">UK DoC, technical file, importer name and address, BS 1363 plug, English instructions</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Great Britain; charger with Bluetooth or Wi-Fi</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">CE or UKCA for the product; radio rules apply</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Above plus Radio Equipment Regulations 2017 evidence</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Northern Ireland; self-declared</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">CE</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">EU or UK technical file that meets NI rules; UKCA alone isn't accepted</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Northern Ireland; UK body third-party assessment</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">CE plus UKNI</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">CE and UKNI marking; cannot be sold in the EU or EEA</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Great Britain and Northern Ireland together</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">CE, plus UKCA if you want it for GB</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">One technical file can support both; check GB designated standards and NI rules</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm bg-white">Great Britain and the EU</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm bg-white">CE, plus UKCA if you want it for GB</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm bg-white">EU DoC for the EU, UK DoC for GB, and CE for both</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>

 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The table is a starting point. The final answer depends on the charger's design, the applicable regulations, and the conformity assessment route.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="ce-recognition" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">2. What Changed in 2024: CE Recognition in Great Britain</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Product Safety and Metrology etc. (Amendment) Regulations 2024 removed the expiry date for CE recognition in Great Britain. The change took effect on 1 October 2024 and covers 21 product regulations, including:</p>
 <ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li>Electrical Equipment (Safety) Regulations 2016</li>
 <li>Electromagnetic Compatibility Regulations 2016</li>
 <li>Radio Equipment Regulations 2017</li>
 <li>Restriction of the Use of Certain Hazardous Substances Regulations 2012</li>
 <li>Ecodesign for Energy-Related Products Regulations 2010</li>
 <li>Machinery, toys, pressure equipment, PPE, gas appliances, and other listed regulations</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For these products, businesses can use either CE or UKCA to place goods on the GB market, provided the relevant UK essential requirements are met. Dual marking is allowed where both sets of conditions are satisfied.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Some sectors aren't in the 21-regulation list: medical devices, construction products, cableways, transportable pressure equipment, rail interoperability, unmanned aircraft systems, and marine equipment. Those sectors have separate arrangements. Chargers aren't in the excluded group, but the product must still fall within the listed regulations.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Why CE isn't automatically a UK presumption of conformity</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Current gov.uk guidance adds an important technical point. CE-marked products that follow EU harmonised standards don't automatically get a presumption of conformity with UK regulations. UKCA-marked products that follow GB designated standards do.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Where a GB designated standard differs from or restricts an EU harmonised standard, a CE-marked manufacturer may need to supply additional technical documentation showing how the UK essential requirements are met. In some cases, a stricter conformity assessment route applies.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For most chargers, the standards overlap: BS EN 62368-1 for safety, and BS EN 55032 and BS EN 55035 for EMC. The practical lesson isn't to assume the EU file is enough. Check that the technical file references the GB designated standards and covers the UK essential requirements.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="2027-easement" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">3. The 31 December 2027 Date Is Not a CE Deadline</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Suppliers and lab blogs often describe 31 December 2027 as the date CE stops being valid in the UK. That's wrong.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The date is the end of a labelling easement. Until 11pm on 31 December 2027, UKCA may be affixed to a label or accompanying document for most sectors, rather than permanently on the product. The same easement allows importer information for EEA and Swiss goods to appear on packaging or accompanying documents.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">It doesn't extend the CE recognition date, because CE recognition in GB no longer has an expiry date for the covered regulations. It doesn't make UKCA mandatory on 1 January 2028. It's a labelling flexibility with a fixed end date.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Sector exceptions apply. Medical devices, construction products, cableways, marine equipment, rail products, transportable pressure equipment, and uncrewed aircraft systems have separate rules.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="gb-vs-ni" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">4. Great Britain vs Northern Ireland</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Great Britain and Northern Ireland are not one market for product marking. Treat them as two files.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Great Britain</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">CE or UKCA is accepted for the covered regulations. Dual marking is allowed. The UK importer must still meet the GB importer duties, even when the product carries CE.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Northern Ireland</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">CE is mandatory. UKCA alone isn't accepted. CE plus UKNI applies only when a UK-based body carried out mandatory third-party conformity assessment. If an EU notified body assessed the product, CE alone is the correct mark.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A product with CE plus UKNI cannot be sold in the EU or EEA. If you sell into both NI and the EU, plan the marking and the assessment route separately.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">The EU</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">UKCA isn't accepted in the EU. The EU market requires CE. A UK Approved Body certificate isn't a substitute for an EU notified body certificate where third-party assessment is required.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="charger-rules" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">5. Charger-Specific UK Rules</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The marking decision is only one part of the file. Chargers have several UK-specific checkpoints that an EU-only compliance packet often misses.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Safety, EMC, radio, and RoHS</h3>
 <ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>Safety:</strong> Electrical Equipment (Safety) Regulations 2016. The usual standard is BS EN 62368-1.</li>
 <li><strong>EMC:</strong> Electromagnetic Compatibility Regulations 2016. The usual standards are BS EN 55032 and BS EN 55035.</li>
 <li><strong>Radio:</strong> Radio Equipment Regulations 2017 if the charger has Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, or another radio function.</li>
 <li><strong>RoHS:</strong> Restriction of the Use of Certain Hazardous Substances Regulations 2012.</li>
 <li><strong>Ecodesign:</strong> Check whether the external power supply or charger falls within the Ecodesign for Energy-Related Products Regulations 2010.</li>
 </ul>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">The BS 1363 plug and BS 1362 fuse</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Plugs and Sockets etc. (Safety) Regulations 1994 require appliances intended for domestic use at 200V or above to be fitted with a BS 1363 standard plug containing a BS 1362 fuse. The fuse rating must match the manufacturer's instructions or the relevant table in BS 1363.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">There's a narrow exception. A non-UK plug may be used if it's fitted with a notified-body-approved conversion plug that encloses the original plug and requires a tool to remove. A travel adaptor doesn't count.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">This is a charger-specific checkpoint. A CE-marked charger with an EU plug isn't automatically ready for the UK shelf. Ask the factory for the UK plug version, the fuse rating, and the plug approval evidence.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">PSTI for connectable products</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If the charger is a connectable product with app or network functionality, check the Product Security and Telecommunications Infrastructure regime. This is design-dependent. Don't assume every charger is in scope.</p>

