<section id="importer-prep" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">6. What a UK Importer Must Prepare</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The importer is the responsible economic operator for the GB market. The factory can prepare documents, but the importer still has to check them and keep them.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">A UK Declaration of Conformity</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The UK DoC should identify the product, the manufacturer or authorised representative, the UK statutory instrument, the GB designated standards used, and the person signing on behalf of the manufacturer. It shouldn't simply reuse the EU DoC with the EU directive name left in.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you place the product on the market under your own name or trademark, or modify it, you may be treated as the manufacturer and take on the full manufacturer obligations. Check your role before you approve the artwork.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">The technical file, retained for 10 years</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The importer must keep the declaration of conformity and technical documentation for 10 years. The file should include the test reports, the conformity assessment evidence, the label artwork, and the instructions. If a market surveillance authority asks for the file, you need the exact model, not a similar one.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Importer identity on the product</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The importer's name, trade name or mark, and postal address must appear on the equipment. If that's not practicable, it may go on the packaging or accompanying documents. For EEA or Swiss goods placed on the GB market before 31 December 2027, the importer details may appear on packaging or an accompanying document.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">English instructions and risk-based checks</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The product needs English instructions. The importer must also carry out risk-based testing and complaint investigations, keep records, take corrective action, notify the market surveillance authority of risks, protect conformity during storage and transport, and cooperate with enforcement.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">A document checklist</h3>
 <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl">
 <table class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl text-sm overflow-hidden">
 <thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white">
 <tr><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Document</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">What to Check</th></tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">UK DoC</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">References the UK statutory instrument and GB designated standards, not only the EU directive</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Test reports</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Match the exact model, plug variant, and radio configuration</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Technical file</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Complete and retained for 10 years</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Importer identity</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Name, trade mark, and postal address on the product or permitted packaging</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Instructions</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">English language, correct model and warnings</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Plug and fuse</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">BS 1363 plug, BS 1362 fuse, or approved conversion plug</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm bg-white">EPR registration</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm bg-white">WEEE and battery producer registration where applicable</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">A typical importer scenario</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Consider Alex, a UK importer who ordered a 65W GaN charger with a CE mark and an EU DoC. The factory's file was complete for the EU, but the DoC cited the Low Voltage Directive and the EMC Directive, not the UK regulations. The charger also arrived with an EU plug.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Alex's retailer asked for a UK DoC and a BS 1363 version. The factory could supply both, but the rework took two weeks and delayed the listing. The product was compliant in principle. The file and the plug weren't ready for the UK market.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The lesson is simple: ask for the UK DoC and the UK plug before the purchase order, not after the container arrives.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="weee-battery" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">7. UK WEEE and Battery Producer Registration</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Marking isn't the only UK obligation. Importing chargers can make you an EEE producer and a battery producer.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">WEEE</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you import electrical and electronic equipment into the UK on a commercial basis, you're an EEE producer. Chargers and power banks fall within EEE categories such as small IT and telecommunications equipment.</p>
 <ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>5 tonnes or less per year:</strong> register directly with the environmental regulator through the WEEE online service by 31 January, or within 28 days of first placing EEE on the market.</li>
 <li><strong>More than 5 tonnes per year:</strong> join an approved producer compliance scheme by 15 November, or within 28 days if you enter the market later.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Producers must report EEE by category and by household or non-household status, keep records for at least four years, mark products with the crossed-out wheeled bin and date mark, and give distributors the producer registration number.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Batteries</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A UK business that is first in the selling chain to make batteries available on the UK market is a battery producer. This includes batteries fitted in imported equipment, such as power banks and chargers with built-in cells.</p>
 <ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>1 tonne or less per year of portable batteries:</strong> register on the NPWD with the regulator for the UK nation where your registered office is, and report by 31 January.</li>
 <li><strong>More than 1 tonne per year:</strong> join a battery compliance scheme by 15 October.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Report battery weight separately from electrical equipment weight, even if the equipment is outside WEEE. Confirm the classification with the regulator if the battery is built in and not removable.</p>

