<!-- 8. CONCLUSION -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section id="conclusion" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">Next Steps: Match the File to the Market</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-700 font-semibold leading-relaxed mb-4">Direct answer: the UKCA vs CE question is not a binary choice. It's a market-by-market file.</p>
 <ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>Great Britain:</strong> CE or UKCA, with a UK DoC, importer identity, BS 1363 plug, and English instructions.</li>
 <li><strong>Northern Ireland:</strong> CE, with CE plus UKNI only after UK-body third-party assessment.</li>
 <li><strong>EU:</strong> CE, with no UKCA substitution.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The practical work is in the documents and the plug, not the logo. Ask your factory for the UK DoC, the GB designated standards, the exact-model test reports, the UK plug version, and the importer details before you approve the order. Then register for WEEE and batteries where the thresholds apply.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

